 {:seon.task/name "one-merge-path"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-25 (docs/decisions.md, root's special cases go): a branch reaches main only through a merge task, whose run the reconciler judges and merges with the writer's octopus merge (`seon.reconcile`'s :octopus-merge, `seon.writer/octopus-merge`). What existed beside it: `seon.root/merge!` (its request-table case, the api's :merge-tests op, `seon.agent-proc`'s gated :merge-tests branch that forked tests then asked the writer's :merge, the writer's :merge op and `merge-branch`, the api's `{:merged ...}` answer and `:merged` event with its render count and stream row (`seon.render.ui/merge-words`), the `:seon.merge/red` error): a second way to change main that skips the done rule, `:behind` handling and the octopus's disjointness, with no production caller. Its users were the smoke, scale and durable check modes and web_test. Deleted all of it (about 70 lines of src). The checks put a branch into main with the one writer op the reconciler sends (`seon.check`'s merge-in!: the agent commits its branch, `seon.agent/commit!`, as a run's end is committed before its merge, then an octopus merge of one member; without the commit durable-write merged nothing, the agent's eval still uncommitted); smoke's `gated by its tests` became `a1-merged` (its tests are already its own check). Correctness: one merge mechanism, the one the reconciler judges. Perf: none on a running root (the path had no caller); the checks' merges no longer fork a child to run tests first (one test fork per merge replaced by one commit eval, in smoke, scale and durable). Proved: bin/prove, bin/check smoke, durable, scale 12 (ten merges), bin/test seon.agent-test seon.root-test green."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.web-test/calls-become-requests seon.web-test/a-refused-ui-call-reaches-the-stream-and-main
                      seon.procs-test/a-writers-merge-names-its-runtime seon.reconcile-test/the-judge-and-the-merge-gate-agree-on-contract-gaps}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-wait-on-main-ends-when-nothing-can-make-it-true"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the stability sweep of 2026-10-25 (notes/stability-2026-10-25.md; the owner: platform stability first). seon.root/await-db parks on a promise that only a later commit satisfying pred delivers (src/seon/root.clj:1013); its callers add a defect after t as a second end (seon_done, src/seon/server.clj:151-170), and test/seon/await.clj's await-quiet rides on it. When root is quiet (no message in flight, every proc parked: the reconciler's :seon.root/quiescent report) and pred does not hold, nothing inside root will ever make it hold, and the wait hangs its caller, the caller's socket and, in a test, the shard (instability 2: 1h45m at 0% CPU). Root stopping does not end it either. Correct, at the producer: root keeps its open waits (await-db's watch keys) and answers each, at the quiescence report that finds pred false, with a typed error {:seon.error/type :seon.root/never-true :t t :quiet-t q} (a fact, never a clock); and at stop! with :seon.root/stopped. A caller that waits on the outside world (a file change, a peer's commit) says so and is not answered at quiet (an explicit :outside? flag, the one exception). seon_done then answers a judge that never finished as that error. judge! (root.clj:1011) puts with >!! on a 64-slot channel and parks forever on a dead, full consumer: the same rule, a put to a proc that died answers its error (see seon.root/a-put-to-a-dead-proc-is-an-error). Proofs: an await-db whose pred cannot hold on a quiet root answers :seon.root/never-true; one waiting at stop! answers :seon.root/stopped; seon_done on a run whose judge's agent died answers the error, never parks."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-put-to-a-dead-proc-is-an-error"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the stability sweep of 2026-10-25 (notes/stability-2026-10-25.md; the owner: platform stability first). >!! onto a bounded channel whose consumer died parks the putter forever once the buffer is full: seon.root/judge! (src/seon/root.clj:1011, 64 slots), src/seon/web.clj:146,148,152,171 toward the web proc, src/seon/fork_server.clj:175-183's event reader into ev-ch. A Flow proc that dies leaves its in-channels open. Correct: a proc's death closes its in-channels (Flow's own stop closes them; a step that threw is the defect path), so a put returns false and the putter records the dead proc as its answer (AGENTS section 4: a dependency is a channel a proc parks on; its death is a fact). Proof: a proc stopped with a full in-channel; a >!! to it returns false and its caller answers an error naming the proc."
  :seon.task/kind :work}]
